{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:FR-02-inv","The factor in a second difference","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> be integers with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span>, and put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>lcm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.\nThen </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>2</mn><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∣</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> To see the\nfactor, set <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi></math></span>. The square\nbound ensures that <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math></span> and\nthat every prime divisor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span> of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> also divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></span>: indeed, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span> implies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math></span>. Consequently <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">gcd</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>, and the displayed difference\nequals </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nAlso <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></msup></math></span>, so <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> is even. The three arguments <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> are therefore odd and greater than\ntwo, and all three totients are even. This proves the extra factor of\ntwo. The divisibility is a lower bound, not an exact valuation at each\nLCM height. The different family in Observation <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:FR-03-kill\">6.108</a> does not satisfy\nthese LCM and square-window hypotheses, so it does not establish\nsharpness within this restricted family.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:CP-01-inv","Carry displacement and tail\nintegrality","<p><em> For a positive integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span> and an integral totient carry <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>v</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The recurrence and the limit identify\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></math></span>, so the difference is\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. This tests\ndivisibility of a carry difference and gives no dimension bound for the\ncarry sections. For another coefficient sequence, the same argument\napplies once its carry is identified with its scaled tail under the\ncorresponding recurrence and limit.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:CP-02","A consequence of rationality and\nits limitation","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>, there are a positive integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span> and an integer carry sequence <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, whose retained sections\nthrough every level <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi></math></span> have rational\nrank at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. At the same\ntime, those sections are uniformly eventually periodic modulo <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span>. Periodicity after reduction modulo\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi></math></span> is a statement in a finite\nquotient; it is not a rank upper bound over <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:D5cons\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:D5cons\">6.76</a> gives the lower bound,\nwhile the rational comparison sequence explains why a general rank upper\nbound cannot be deduced from these recurrence assumptions\nalone.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:CP-05-inv","A totient difference congruent to\ntwo modulo four","<p><em> For every positive integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi></math></span> and every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>, there is a prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>B</mi></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≡</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nTo construct <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span>, set <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>h</mi></math></span>, choose a prime <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, and use Dirichlet’s\ntheorem in the progression <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. This progression is\nreduced: its residue is odd and is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>≢</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, so <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≡</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, whereas\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span> gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>4</mn><mo>∣</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Subtracting gives the\nstated congruence. This supplies the arithmetic congruence at\narbitrarily large primes; the additional finite exclusion in\nProposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:CP-07\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:CP-07\">6.70</a>\nremains a separate hypothesis.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:TA-inv","A prescribed residue after a long\ndivisibility block","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> be integers. For every\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> there is a prime\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" columnalign=\"right left right left\" columnspacing=\"0em 2em 0em\" rowspacing=\"3pt\" data-break-align=\"bottom top bottom top\" minlabelspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mi>p</mi></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd><mtd><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mstyle></mtd><mtd><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></mtd><mtd><mstyle indentshift=\"2em\"><mi></mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The construction uses <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> distinct auxiliary primes congruent\nto <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.444em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>: one for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>, and two for each of the <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> preceding positions. Choose them\nlarger than <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. For the\nfirst prime impose <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≡</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>; for\nthe pair at position <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> impose <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>j</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>, respectively. These nonzero\nresidues, together with the displayed odd residue modulo <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></math></span>, combine by the Chinese remainder\ntheorem into a reduced progression. Dirichlet’s theorem then supplies\narbitrarily large prime values of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi></math></span>. Each auxiliary prime contributes the\nrequired factor <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></math></span> to the\ncorresponding totient.</em></p>\n<p><em>Thus the first <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> totient\ndifferences in the window starting at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> vanish modulo <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></math></span>, while the last is <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> modulo <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></math></span>. Consequently </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≡</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> If\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> for every\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>, choose such a prime with\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span>. Iterating the carry\nrecurrence from <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> then gives\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"1em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width=\"0.333em\" linebreak=\"nobreak\"></mspace><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The initial integer is\nmultiplied by <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></math></span> and disappears\nonly after reduction modulo <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></msup></math></span>.\nThis does not bound its higher quotient. The condition <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> is part of the construction; it\ndoes not provide arbitrarily large <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi></math></span> for one fixed shift <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:MP-01-inv","A Möbius-inversion formula for the\ntail","<p><em></em></p>\n<p><em>For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Thus <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is the distance to the next\nstrictly larger multiple of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span>, so\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span>. Möbius inversion\ngives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed,\nsubstitute <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span> and group by <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span>. Its multiples after <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span> have quotients <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>ℓ</mi></math></span> and forward distances <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>ℓ</mi></math></span>, for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>; summing the two geometric\nseries gives the formula. Absolute convergence justifies the regrouping,\nsince the sum of the absolute divisor contributions at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span> is at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></span>. This\nidentity is available for later transformations; it does not assert that\nevery Möbius or Lambert-series argument must use it.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:SK-01-inv","Explicit finite examples","<p><em> The\nfinite certificates <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>23</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>64</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>64</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>93</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> imply <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>. Both\nsatisfy the short-window restriction, since <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>23</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>32</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>93</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>128</mn></math></span>. They are the certificates at\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></math></span> in the diagonal table of\nSection <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#ssec:certtable\">5.7</a>. These\nare two explicit instances of the desired condition, not the start of a\nproved induction or a claim that no other finite instances can be\nchecked.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:A5-inv","The necessary depth","<p><em> Every\ncertificate <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>\nsatisfies </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The\ntwo endpoint margins must fit inside an interval of length <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></msup></math></span>. In particular, successful depths\ngrow at least logarithmically with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></math></span>. This is a necessary lower bound, not\nan upper bound for the first successful depth. The later two-bit\nreformulation applies at its stated modulus and does not reverse this\nimplication.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:A9-inv","Rationality gives an eventual tail\nperiod","<p><em> Suppose <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></msup><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>\nin lowest terms, where <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> is odd. Take <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, including <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>. Euler’s theorem gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>v</mi><mo>∣</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, so </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThe identity relating <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span> to its\nscaled tails therefore gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></math></span>. These are explicit witnesses for\nthe eventual period asserted in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:A10\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:A10\">6.71</a>. To use the finite residue\ntest against a hypothetical rational value, one must obtain a\nnonintegral difference for its period at an index beyond its preperiod.\nSoundness proves that the quantified certificate condition rules out\nrationality. Conversely, the prefix-tail identity makes every\npositive-shift tail difference nonintegral when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span> is irrational, and pointwise\ncompleteness (Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:a7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:a7\">6.19</a>) then supplies a\ndepth. Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:AR-06-inv\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:AR-06-inv\">6.81</a> only identifies the\nweighted diagonal sum with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span>; it is\nnot the completeness argument and supplies no depth bound.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:B6-inv","Tail integrality on an LCM grid","<p><em> If <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>, there is\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> such that for all\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The\nthreshold may depend on the hypothetical rational value. A certificate\nat one fixed scale therefore gives a finite denominator exclusion, not\nirrationality. Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:B7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:B7\">6.73</a> requires certificates at\narbitrarily large scales, so that one lies beyond the threshold supplied\nby any such rational value.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:D4-inv","Independence of the retained dyadic\nfamily","<p><em> For every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, the\nauxiliary canonical dyadic family has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> indexed sections, and these\nsections are linearly independent over <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>. The CRT–Dirichlet argument is\nexplained in Section <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#sec:mahler-defect\">10.8</a>. For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> the family is a basis for all\nsections through level <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi></math></span>. At <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> the auxiliary family still contains\nboth <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, so it is not the actual\nlevel-zero truncation, whose dimension is one. No rationality hypothesis\non <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span> is used.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:C2-inv","The exact range of the stated\nFarey-gap inequality","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>V</mi></math></span>\nbe the totient residue at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>240</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> defined in Section <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#ssec:farey\">5.3</a>. For every integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi></math></span> satisfying </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>79639646646701375323355774875831053</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> one has </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>240</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>243</mn><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>240</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The\nfirst failure occurs at </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>79639646646701375323355774875831054</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nobtained from the stated Farey mediant. Thus the range is exact for this\nparticular inequality. It is not an optimality claim among all\ndenominator-exclusion tests. The preceding range gives the exclusion in\nProposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:C3-inv\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:C3-inv\">6.100</a>,\napproximately <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>7.96</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:C3-inv","The resulting denominator\nexclusion","<p><em> For every reduced fraction <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>79639646646701375323355774875831053</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>S</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This is an unconditional\nfinite denominator exclusion. It does not assume certificates at\narbitrarily large scales, as required by Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:A10\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:A10\">6.71</a>. Conversely, excluding\nthis finite range does not rule out rational values with larger reduced\ndenominators.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:D1D2-inv","Two general irrationality\ncriteria","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></math></span>.\nOne sufficient condition is a sequence of reduced fractions <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>x</mi></math></span> for all sufficiently large <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span> and </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟶</mo><mn>0.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Another is\nthat, for every integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>,\nthere exist <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span> such\nthat </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed,\nif <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span> is reduced with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, every nonzero error in the first\ncondition is at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, and\nevery nonzero error in the second is at least <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span>. The strict lower bounds exclude\nexact rational hits. Requiring <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></math></span> for a fixed integer base <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> is a stronger sufficient\ncondition, not a reformulation valid for every irrational number. The\nbinary example in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:d2\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d2\">6.23</a> explains this\ndistinction.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:D7-inv","Lambert identities involving <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi></math></span>","<p><em> For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, the\nidentities are <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi></math></span>. The last identity\nrewrites the same unknown value; it does not deduce its irrationality\nfrom that of <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi></math></span>. The value <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>Id</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup></math></span>\nis transcendental by Nesterenko <span class=\"citation\"\ndata-cites=\"nesterenko1996\"><a href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#ref-13\">[13]</a></span>. These examples show that\nLambert-series form alone does not determine arithmetic status. The\nindividual comparisons are in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#catalogue:cert:d7\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#catalogue:cert:d7\">6.43</a> and its\ntable.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:D9-inv","A general rational gap bound","<p><em>\nIf <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span> are reduced fractions\nwith <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> since\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></math></span> is a positive integer. In\nparticular, if <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>S</mi></math></span> is a reduced rational\napproximation with positive error at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></math></span>, then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> These\nlower bounds tend to infinity precisely when <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>; a subsequence with\nthis property is enough to contradict a fixed <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi></math></span>. A tail bound by itself does not imply\nthis. For example, the ordinary dyadic prefix has denominator dividing\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></math></span> and tail at most <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></math></span>. Using only these two bounds\nyields <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, which is\nvacuous. The rational-spacing lemma can convert sharper error and\ndenominator information into an exclusion; it does not automatically\nimprove the Farey bound in Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.html#prop:C2-inv\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface#prop:C2-inv\">6.99</a>.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos249-totient-reasoning-surface.json?v=ba7ef333d530"}
